@@ -72,6 +72,8 @@ Each inner transaction contains the metadata for its own processing. Only the in
 
 There is also a pointer back to the outer transaction (`ParentBatchID`).
 
+For `ALLORNOTHING`, if any inner transaction fails, none of the inner transactions are committed. Their hashes can return `txnNotFound` from `tx`; there is no committed inner metadata containing the failure code. The outer `tesSUCCESS` confirms only outer sequence and fee processing. Before treating missing inner hashes as a rollback, verify that the server has the complete validated ledger containing the outer transaction. Check all expected inner hashes in that ledger; a missing response from an unavailable server or one with incomplete history is not proof of rollback. Do not report purchase success or blindly submit a new payment based on the outer result alone.
+
 
 ## Security for XRPL Batch Transactions
 
@@ -111,7 +113,7 @@ Inner transactions cannot be broadcast (and won't be accepted if they happen to
 
 - Since the outer transaction returns `tesSUCCESS` even when inner transactions fail (see [Metadata](#metadata)), you must check each inner transaction's metadata and result codes to determine its actual outcome.
 - If inner transactions are validated, they are included in the same ledger as the outer transaction. If an inner transaction appears in a different ledger, it is likely a fraud attempt.
-- Systems that don't specifically handle `Batch` transactions should be able to support them without any changes, since each inner transaction is a valid transaction on its own. All inner transactions that have a `tes` (success) or `tec` result code are accessible via standard transaction-fetching mechanisms such as [`tx`](/docs/references/http-websocket-apis/public-api-methods/transaction-methods/tx.md) and [`account_tx`](/docs/references/http-websocket-apis/public-api-methods/account-methods/account_tx.md).
+- Systems that don't specifically handle `Batch` transactions should be able to support them without any changes, since each inner transaction is a valid transaction on its own. All **committed** inner transactions that have a `tes` (success) or `tec` result code are accessible via standard transaction-fetching mechanisms such as [`tx`](/docs/references/http-websocket-apis/public-api-methods/transaction-methods/tx.md) and [`account_tx`](/docs/references/http-websocket-apis/public-api-methods/account-methods/account_tx.md).
 - In a multi-account `Batch` transaction, the batch signers sign the outer transaction's account, sequence, and batch mode flags, as well as each inner transaction hash; changing any of these afterward invalidates the signatures. The fee is the only part of the outer transaction that is _not_ signed, so the submitter can set it without coordinating with the other parties.
 
 The following sections cover additional recommendations for specific types of integrations.
diff --git a/docs/references/protocol/transactions/types/batch.md b/docs/references/protocol/transactions/types/batch.md
